```cs
// @@@ If the return value is true, the last position of x is atleast arrListIndexOfX in the new array
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX, "Last position of x should be at least arrListIndexOfX after an operation that adds x to the array.");
```
This checks if the index of the last occurrence of x in the array is less than or equal to the index of the last occurrence of x in the array after the operation that adds x. If this is not the case, it means that, for example, the array has been overwritten from before, which could be considered an internal error.

The `Contains` method can have different behavior depending on the data type it's operating on. For more precise and comprehensive assertions, you should write out the behavior specified in the documentation of the `Contains` method. In this case, since it's an array or list you assume a similar behavior as `Contains` returns `true` if the parameter is contained in the array/list (same as index >= 0).

