Note: The code may contain a typo, it is not written with NUnit.Framework.Assert but with Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting.Assert.--------------------```cs
[Test]
public void TestMethod()
{
    var list = new ArrayList();
    var x = 10;
    list.Add(x);

    Assert.NotNull(list);
    Assert.True(list.Contains(x));

    var index = list.IndexOf(x);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, index);

    // If the return value is true, this is the last position of x
    var lastIndex = list.LastIndexOf(x);
    Assert.True(index <= lastIndex);

    Assert.AreEqual(1, list.Count);

    list.Remove(x);
    Assert.False(list.Contains(x));
    Assert.AreEqual(0, list.Count);

    list.Insert(0, x);
    Assert.True(list.Contains(x));
    Assert.AreEqual(1, list.Count);

    list[0] = x; // set item
    Assert.AreEqual(x, list[0]);
}

```
The `Assert` statements created based on the comments are in the output. The `Assert.NotNull(list)`, `Assert.True(list.Contains(x))` and `Assert.AreEqual(1, list.Count)` are added to ensure the elements are added and correctly count. `Assert.AreEqual(0, index)` is added to ensure that the first position is index 0. `Assert.False(list.Contains(x))` and `Assert.AreEqual(0, list.Count)` are there to verify removal of elements. `Assert.True(list.Contains(x))`, `Assert.AreEqual(1, list.Count)`, `Assert.AreEqual(x, list[0])` are there to verify insertion and changes to elements.--------------------```cs
